  "transcript": "# United Nations Industrial Development Organization\n\n## Why the UK left UNIDO (2010–12)\n> The UK withdrew because a formal review judged UNIDO had poor impact on poverty, weak country presence, and weak financial transparency.\n\nChair: Sir Andrew, thank you for joining us. You led the decision to leave UNIDO in 2010. Could you explain why the UK withdrew and whether you now regret it?\nSir Andrew Mitchell: When we formed government in 2010 we set a high bar for development spending. Parliament backed 0.7% of GNI for aid, so taxpayers needed to see clear results.\nSir Andrew Mitchell: We created the multilateral aid review to test 43 UN agencies against UK interests and value for money. The public and experts could see the findings.\nSir Andrew Mitchell: UNIDO fell into the group we could no longer justify funding. Key problems were: little evidence of impact on global poverty, a weak country-level presence, and poor financial management and transparency.\nSir Andrew Mitchell: We withdrew membership over two years and saved roughly £7 million a year in membership fees. That didn’t stop funding specific projects where needed.\nChair: So the decision was public and tested by experts. You say project funding could still continue even after leaving?\nSir Andrew Mitchell: Yes. We stopped core membership payments but could (and did) pay for targeted projects when there was a clear UK interest or urgent need.\n\n\n## Have reforms made UNIDO acceptable now?\n> There have been real reforms—more focus on green industry, better transparency and project work—but serious gaps in results measurement and accountability remain.\n\nBaroness Anelay of St Johns: UNIDO now has a new director-general and a different focus. Has it genuinely reformed enough to justify the UK rejoining?\nSir Andrew Mitchell: There are three important changes since my time. First, UNIDO now emphasises industrial decarbonisation and green supply chains — that’s closer to current UK priorities.\nSir Andrew Mitchell: Second, oversight and transparency have improved: it has an independent evaluation unit and claims to be leaner and more open than before.\nSir Andrew Mitchell: Third, the UK has shifted to funding specific UNIDO programmes — for example clean technology projects — rather than core membership in recent years.\nChair: So some reforms are real. Are there still management problems?\nSir Andrew Mitchell: Yes. UNIDO’s own 2018–25 review found results management wasn’t fully implemented, data systems are fragmented, baselines incomplete, and accountability below senior levels is weak.\nSir Andrew Mitchell: A nicer strategy and better units don’t prove that taxpayers’ money is delivering outcomes. That’s the crux of the remaining concern.\n\n\n## Money, membership and practical projects\n> Membership fees were small but membership gives governance influence; large project funding is handled by other UK departments, and claimed leverage must be checked.\n\nChair: You mentioned saving £7 million a year. Is that the membership fee we stopped paying?\nSir Andrew Mitchell: Yes — roughly £7 million annually was the cost of board membership that we cut when we withdrew.\nChair: But the UK has since channelled much larger sums through UNIDO for projects — we heard figures like £125m rising toward £200m. How does that fit?\nSir Andrew Mitchell: I don’t have exact numbers to hand. That programme-level money is not from the Foreign Office’s budget; much of it comes via DESNZ and specific climate or investment programmes.\nBaroness Lawlor: Is the UK better off running targeted bilateral projects ourselves, or funding UNIDO programmes?\nSir Andrew Mitchell: Both approaches have merits. Bilateral work gives direct oversight — boots on the ground — but UNIDO can sometimes unlock larger finance if projects are well run. Each case must be judged for value and risk.\nSir Andrew Mitchell: For example, a UK-funded UNIDO Brazil project used £15m of UK money to supposedly unlock £250m of development finance and projected private investment, though those leverage claims need verification.\nBaroness Lawlor: So the leverage is promising but not yet proven?\nSir Andrew Mitchell: Exactly. That’s why independent evaluation matters before scaling up or tying membership to large spending commitments.\nLord Hannay of Chiswick: Being on UNIDO’s governing board gives the UK a voice in decisions. But if we are a minority we could be tied to collective choices we don't like. Is that a concern?\nSir Andrew Mitchell: Yes. Board membership partly implicates you in decisions. ‘Seat at the table’ arguments are attractive but must be tested against whether the influence gained justifies the cost and risks.\n\n\n## Checks, reviews and parliamentary scrutiny\n> Rejoining should be coupled to clear, independent evaluation, use MAR-type criteria, a firm 2029 review date, and parliamentary oversight to hold government to account.\n\nLord Hannay of Chiswick: Should the rejoining decision be formally reviewed later, and should Parliament be involved to strengthen our negotiating position?\nSir Andrew Mitchell: Yes. The Government has proposed a 2029 review. That must be more than a gesture — it should be independent, use clear criteria and be available for parliamentary committees to scrutinise.\nSir Andrew Mitchell: Parliamentary scrutiny is an asset. It gives negotiators leverage to demand credible reporting and evidence of results from UNIDO.\nLord Stevenson of Balmacara: If we use the original MAR as a model, is that still appropriate given how UK capacity has changed since 2010?\nSir Andrew Mitchell: The MAR’s core questions remain useful: does the agency deliver development impact, value for money, additionality, good governance and sustainability? But the review must recognise current limits in UK bilateral capacity and draw on external experts.\nBaroness Lawlor: Who should do the review and how do we avoid a rubber-stamp exercise?\nSir Andrew Mitchell: Commission independent experts, including academics and practitioners. Officials will be involved, but external evaluators should lead terms of reference and assessments, and Parliament should get the findings.\nSir Andrew Mitchell: If UNIDO fails to meet standards on results and transparency, the UK must be prepared to reconsider membership — that’s what the MAR taught us.\nChair: Thank you. Your position is cautious: UNIDO has improved but not fully met MAR concerns; rejoining must be justified by verified results, a 2029 review and parliamentary scrutiny.\nSir Andrew Mitchell: Yes. Given a constrained ODA budget and the need to prioritise humanitarian and high-impact work, any decision to spend more through UNIDO must be robustly justified and reviewed.\nChair: That is very helpful. No final points?\nSir Andrew Mitchell: No — just that your committee should press for a strong, independent 2029 review and insist on transparent reporting against MAR-style criteria.\nChair: Thank you, Sir Andrew — we are grateful for your time and the clarity of your advice.\n",
